*.iml
.gradle/
.kotlin/
/local.properties
.idea/
.DS_Store
/build
/captures
.externalNativeBuild
.cxx
local.properties
/app/build

# A track export is a precise trace of where someone walked. The repository is public; no GPX
# ever goes in it (owner decision, timestamp-filter dispatch). The four exports behind
# docs/audits/2026-09-06-*-findings.md are the last unfiltered evidence and live off version control.
*.gpx

# The beta-and-release signing identity never enters the repository (dedicated-beta-signing-identity
# dispatch, owner decision): the owner generates and holds the keystore, and its passwords live in
# an untracked signing.properties or in environment variables -- see resolveSigningIdentity() in
# app/build.gradle.kts. The one committed keystore is app/debug.keystore, the debug identity only.
*.jks
*.keystore
!app/debug.keystore
/signing.properties

# Claude Code session state (worktrees, local agent config) -- tooling, never part of a build.
# Untracked entries here previously made resolveBuildIdentity() stamp release builds `.dirty`,
# because the dirty check is `git status --porcelain`, which counts untracked files. That
# sensitivity is deliberate and must not be loosened -- an untracked .kt under src/ IS compiled in,
# so a false *clean* would be the worse failure. Ignore what genuinely cannot affect a build.
#
# `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/`, so that the one negation below can apply: git does not descend
# into an excluded *directory*, so `!.claude/skills/` under a plain `.claude/` rule would never be
# reached. Everything else under .claude -- worktrees, settings, local agent config -- stays ignored
# exactly as before; this widens nothing but the single path named next.
.claude/*
# Skills are prose the project keeps deliberately: they are read by a session and by a person, they
# are shared across machines, and they are the kind of thing a fresh checkout should already have.
# Tracking them does not weaken the dirty check above -- that check counts *untracked* files, so a
# committed skill reads clean where an untracked one would stamp a release `.dirty`. Leaving these
# ignored and uncommitted was the state that could not be kept: the files exist on disk, so they
# would have been permanently invisible to it.
!.claude/skills/

# Written by Gradle's daemon-JVM feature, machine-local.
gradle/gradle-daemon-jvm.properties

# Cygwin/MSYS crash dumps, produced when a tool in the Git-for-Windows toolchain aborts.
*.stackdump
